# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/
# Test artefacts: claim-log probes written by the domain-regression tests
_*probe_*.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
env/
build/
dist/

# Python packaging: the cdylib staged into the wheel by python/build_wheel.py, and its
# provenance record. DESIGN.md §7.8 forbids a checked-in binary because silent drift from
# its source changes what runs; a wheel is a binary from the consumer's point of view, so
# it is a BUILD PRODUCT here and never a tree artifact. `git status` after a wheel build is
# the check on that. The provenance record is what makes the drift detectable once the
# binary has left the tree.
python/src/onnxruntime_ep_vulkan/_lib/
python/dist/

# Squad: ignore runtime state (logs, inbox, sessions)
# Note: .squad/casting/* is identity and MUST be committed to main, not ignored
.squad/orchestration-log/
.squad/log/
.squad/decisions/inbox/
.squad/sessions/
.squad/.scratch/
.squad/.cache/
# Squad: SubSquad activation file (local to this machine)
.squad-workstream

# Bench: transient Vulkan device-probe scratch dirs
bench/vkfacts-*/

# Bench: probe taps and per-arm counters. These are large .npy dumps of intermediate
# activations rewritten by every probe run; the findings that depend on them live in the
# committed bench/results/*.json records, which carry the numbers rather than the arrays.
bench/scratch/

# Test: local pytest capture files
tests/_run_*.txt

# ORT profiling artifacts (written to CWD by enable_profiling)
onnxruntime_profile__*.json
vulkan_test*.json
island_attribution.json
trace_*.json

# CI lane evidence (criterion 10 gate artifacts, Link 2026-07-31).
# Written by ci/gate_chain_fp32.py into bench/results/ci-lane/ on a runner and
# uploaded as a build artifact. Never committed: probe output goes to
# bench/results/ or a temp path, never the repo root.
bench/results/ci-lane/
bench/results/link-gate/
gate_chain_fp32_profile_*.json
bench/results/claim_log_attribution.jsonl

# Written by ci/check_landing_simulation.py on a runner and uploaded as a build
# artifact (Tank 2026-08-07, issue #60). Not committed: it names the base and head
# shas of one particular run, so a committed copy is stale the moment main moves and
# reads as a claim about a landing nobody simulated.
bench/results/landing-simulation.json

# Mouse: models fetched for op-census work. Large, and reproducible from the URL recorded in
# bench/results/model_provenance.json — an artifact belongs in the repo, a 14 MB blob does not.
.scratch-models/

# Raw tensor dumps from probes: regenerable, and one kv.npz reached 2.8 GB.
# They are inputs to a verdict, not the verdict; the JSON records are the evidence.
bench/results/**/*.npy
bench/results/**/*.npz


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CUDA competition harness (issue #69). Mouse 2026-08-07.
#
# `git add -A` in this worktree would otherwise stage a large number of local paths and
# a large volume of data -- neither figure is witnessed by anything committed, and the
# exact counts are machine- and run-specific, so none is quoted here.
# Every rule below names a thing that is *regenerable from a committed record*;
# nothing here is evidence. The evidence is the small JSON/MD pair each run
# writes at the top of bench/results/<record>/, and those stay committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The local CUDA-12 virtualenv the harness runs onnxruntime-gpu from -- a large local
# artifact, not witnessed by anything committed, so no specific size is quoted here.
# It is a machine-local toolchain, not a source artifact: bench/cuda_competition.py
# records the interpreter, the ORT version and the CUDA build flags into every
# result record, which is what makes a run reproducible. A committed venv would
# not be reproducible, only large.
.venv-cu12/

# Per-run worker scratch. cuda_competition.py gives each arm/repeat its own
# directory under the record dir and reduces it to JSON before returning, so the
# directory is an intermediate: a large local artifact (claim logs, counters
# dumps, ORT profiles, output tensors), not witnessed by anything committed, so
# no specific size or file count is quoted here.
bench/results/**/scratch/
bench/results/**/scratch_*/

# Bench: scratch trees written outside bench/ by `--scratch`. The flag takes an
# arbitrary path, and a repo-root directory is the obvious thing to pass, so the
# bench/-anchored rules above do not reach it. These hold per-run .npy inputs and
# raw counter dumps -- transient by construction and hundreds of MB per sweep.
/_bench_scratch/
/_scratch/

# Device-probe records for the CUDA arm. Regenerated by `python -m bench.cuda_probe`;
# each carries a generated .onnx model and raw ORT profiling JSON. The probe's
# conclusion is copied into the competition record's device_facts.
bench/results/_cuda_probe/
bench/results/_cuda_probe_cu12/

# Worker-side per-process artifacts, wherever they land. These are named with the
# worker pid (and, for ORT profiles, a wall-clock stamp), so a committed copy can
# never be matched to anything: the pid is meaningless outside the run that made it.
# The committed record carries the reduced numbers instead.
bench/results/**/outputs_*/
bench/results/**/result_*.json
bench/results/**/claim_vulkan_*.jsonl
bench/results/**/claim_cuda_*.jsonl
bench/results/**/claim_cpu_*.jsonl
bench/results/**/counters_vulkan_*_[0-9]*.json
bench/results/**/profile_vulkan_*_[0-9]*_*.json
bench/results/**/profile_cuda_*_[0-9]*_*.json
bench/results/**/profile_cpu_*_[0-9]*_*.json

# Throwaway one-off analysis scripts written while chasing a number. If a check is
# worth keeping it becomes a test in bench/test_*.py; if it is not, it should not
# outlive the session that needed it.
bench/_diag_*.py
